        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> [@lithammer](https://github.com/lithammer), I did have a thought on not showing peek window if the range is the whole buffer. But decided not to do that for consistency reasons.\n> \n> But I see how this can conflict with `inccommand=split`. At the very least, adding something like `config.autopeek.predicate` might be useful here. The default can then not peek if `inccommand=split` is set and the command uses it.\n> \n> I'll think about it.\n\n@lithammer, there is now [`config.autopeek.predicate`](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/c889667a9d73b106bd303a043eb37a91da4a41a2/doc/mini-cmdline.txt#L133-L134) which by default [doesn't show peek window](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/c889667a9d73b106bd303a043eb37a91da4a41a2/doc/mini-cmdline.txt#L343-L345) if current command can preview its result. Thanks for describing the use case!\n\n> autocorrect seems not comptiable with cabbrev, defined a cabbrev by `vim.keymap.set(\"ca\", \"F\", \"Flogsplit\", {})`, without`mini.cmdline`, run `:F` will run `:Flogsplit`, with `mini.cmdline`, run `:F` will run `:f`.\n\n@gh-liu, word that matches LHS of Command-line abbreviation is now [not corrected](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/c889667a9d73b106bd303a043eb37a91da4a41a2/doc/mini-cmdline.txt#L211). Thanks for mentioning this! I did intend to account for Command-line abbreviations *somehow*, but forgot.",
